Parallel N64 (https://github.com/libretro/parallel-n64, GNU General Public License version 2) runs the Nintendo 64,
and like every libretro core it says nothing of the instructions it runs. The tools apply this change of Dreamports'
own to its source before building it on the contributor's computer (tools/consoles.mjs, tools/libretro/build.mjs), so
the program that plays the original (tools/libretro/host.c) can ask to be told of each instruction its cached
interpreter is about to run, the processor the tools play every game on: its address, and where that is in the
console's memory. That's how prove knows which of the code map's functions ran in each frame, and so which functions
each part of a game runs (tools/lib/part-functions.mjs).

The interpreter only calls the function it was given, when it was given one, just before it runs an instruction:
nothing of the console changes, so every frame's picture and sound are the same with it or without, which
tools/test/n64-kit.mjs checks. Its own steps that are no instruction of the game, the end of a block of instructions
and code it hasn't read yet, aren't told: each goes on to the instruction it stands for, which is told there, so every
instruction is told once. Nothing of Parallel N64 is in the repository but the lines of its own this change sits
between.

It also puts one thing right in Parallel N64 itself, which kept it from running at all on Windows and on a Mac with
Apple's chip as the tools build it, with no dynarec, so every computer runs the same interpreter. A frame ends when the
video interface's interrupt sets a flag that stops the processor, and the next frame starts by clearing it. There are
two such flags, the processor's own and one the dynarec keeps, and which one the interrupt looks at is chosen by the
compiler's own name for the computer: with MinGW on Windows for x86-64, and on arm64, it's the dynarec's, which a
build with no dynarec never cleared. So the first frame ended, the flag stayed set, the interrupt took every later
frame for one already stopped, and the emulator never came back from its second frame. The change clears that flag
too. On Linux for x86-64 the two are one, so nothing changes there, and no frame's picture or sound changes anywhere.

diff --git a/libretro/libretro.c b/libretro/libretro.c
index 4302bb4..5bf8703 100644
--- a/libretro/libretro.c
+++ b/libretro/libretro.c
@@ -3753,6 +3753,25 @@ static void glsm_enter(void)
 #define RETRO_AV_ENABLE_AUDIO (1 << 1)
 #endif
 
+/* Dreamports: the cached interpreter tells the tools' program of every instruction (cached_interp.c), with where its
+ * address is in the console's memory, as an offset from its first byte, by whichever of the processor's two fixed
+ * ways of seeing it, cached at 0x80000000 or not at 0xA0000000, or 0xFFFFFFFF anywhere else, as in an address only the
+ * TLB maps. retro_dreamports_ran_at gives the function to tell, or none. */
+extern void (*DreamportsRan)(uint32_t address);
+static void (*dreamports_told)(uint32_t address, uint32_t place);
+
+static void dreamports_ran(uint32_t address)
+{
+   uint32_t place = address & 0x1FFFFFFF;
+   dreamports_told(address, address >= 0x80000000 && address < 0xC0000000 && place < 0x800000 ? place : 0xFFFFFFFF);
+}
+
+RETRO_API void retro_dreamports_ran_at(void (*told)(uint32_t, uint32_t))
+{
+   dreamports_told = told;
+   DreamportsRan   = told ? dreamports_ran : NULL;
+}
+
 void retro_run (void)
 {
    static bool updated = false;
